## Reliability

_Recover from failure, scale with demand, and keep working when a part
fails._

- Two Availability Zones: at least two instances, one per zone, and the load
  balancer in both.
- Replace what fails: the Auto Scaling group uses the load balancer's health
  check, so an instance that stops answering `GET /health` is replaced;
  systemd restarts a server that exits.
  - `AwsSolutions-AS2`: the group uses ELB health checks.
  - `AwsSolutions-AS1`: the group has a cooldown.
- Scale with demand: a target-tracking policy on CPU between the minimum and
  the maximum the stack sets.
- Change safely: a rolling update replaces one instance at a time, starting
  the new one before the old one stops, so two stay in service; a stopping
  instance gets 30 seconds to finish its requests (deregistration delay,
  `SIGTERM`). CloudFormation waits for each new instance's resource signal,
  which its boot script sends with `cfn-signal` once the server answers
  `/health`: a boot that fails, a server that never answers, or no signal
  within 15 minutes fails the update, and CloudFormation rolls the stack
  back to the instances it had. The CloudFormation endpoint in the VPC
  carries the signal.
- Instances hold no data (`docs/style/aws.md`): what the service keeps is in
  the table. An instance's root volume is deleted with it and never backed
  up, so losing an instance loses nothing but the requests it was
  answering.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: every instance volume is deleted with its
    instance, and no backup plan selects them.
- Recovery objectives (REL13-BP01): `recovery.rpo` and `recovery.rto` say
  what a restore in production may lose and how long it may take;
  `docs/runbook.md` is written to them.
- Back up data (REL09), in production:
  - Point-in-time recovery on the table: any second of the last 35 days, up
    to about five minutes before now, restored to a new table. It meets an
    RPO of minutes.
  - An AWS Backup plan (REL09-BP03): a daily backup at 05:00 UTC of the
    stack's table, named by its ARN, kept `retentionDays` in a vault with
    its own key (REL09-BP02); with `copyToRegion`, each copied to a vault in
    that region (the `<name>-backup-copy` stack), so a lost region loses no
    more than a day (REL13-BP02, backup and restore). A new table joins the
    plan's selection in the stack: nothing is backed up by a tag, which the
    instances, their volumes and a development stack's table carry too.
  - No vault lock: in compliance mode it can never be undone, which is the
    account owner's decision.
  - Development backs up nothing: its data goes with its stack.
- Nothing of value is deleted by accident, in production: the table is
  `RETAIN` with deletion protection, and the stack has termination
  protection. A change that replaces the table (a new key) keeps the old one
  but leaves it outside the stack, empty of the new one's data: review
  every change to a table's key as a migration.
  - `AwsSolutions-DDB3`: point-in-time recovery; `infra/standard.ts`
    acknowledges it in development only.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: per tier, the removal policies, the
    protections, point-in-time recovery, the backup plan, its vault and its
    copy.
  - cfn-lint I3011: every stateful resource has a deletion policy.
- Restore by a written procedure (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP05):
  `docs/runbook.md` restores the items to a point in time, from the vault,
  from the copy, and a deleted table, and rolls a deployment back.
- Idempotency: a retried request changes nothing twice (whole-item puts,
  conditional writes, idempotency keys).
- Limits: a body size cap, keep-alive longer than the load balancer's idle
  timeout, and a request answered within it.
- By hand, and why: a restore drill (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP03), at least a
  quarter apart, and a game day once a year; only an account shows that a
  restore works and how long it takes, so the RTO is a promise until a drill
  measures it. Whether a rolling update stops on a bad instance, too, only
  a deploy shows. `docs/runbook.md` says how.

## Performance efficiency

_Use resources efficiently as demand changes and technology evolves._

- The newest Amazon Linux 2023 image and an LTS Node.js from its
  repositories.
- Size the instance to the work: measure before choosing a bigger type or a
  higher minimum.
- Start-up: SDK clients created once; the server bundled into one file by
  esbuild, so an instance starts without `npm install`.
- Data access by key: `GetItem` and `Query` on keys and indexes; a `Scan` is
  never on a request path.
- Find where time goes with the p99 latency alarm and the load balancer's
  access logs.
- By hand: measure a stated budget with a benchmark, and say what only a
  deployed service can show (latency through the load balancer, scaling
  time).

## Cost optimization

_Deliver value at the lowest price: pay only for what you use, and know
where the money goes._

- Instances, the load balancer and the two interface endpoints (Logs,
  CloudFormation) cost by the hour whether or not there is work: keep the
  minimum at two and the type small unless the issue states the load that
  needs more.
- No NAT gateway: gateway endpoints for S3 and DynamoDB cost nothing, and
  two interface endpoints cost less than a NAT gateway per zone.
- `arm64` (Graviton) instances: cheaper than x86 for the same work.
- What production costs over development: point-in-time recovery on the
  table (by its size, per month); AWS Backup's storage (each daily backup is
  a full copy of the table, kept `retentionDays`, so about that many times
  the table's size), and with `copyToRegion` the transfer of each copy and
  its storage there; twelve months of logs and access logs instead of one;
  and a KMS key per vault. The instances, the load balancer, the endpoints,
  the alarms (each per month) and the dashboard cost the same in both
  tiers. Lower `retentionDays` to pay less for backups; 0 keeps
  point-in-time recovery alone.
- On-demand DynamoDB and log retention limit what storage costs grow to
  (`infra/standard.test.ts`, cfn-lint I3013).
- Tags attribute every cost to its project, system and container, once the
  account activates them as cost allocation tags.
- By hand, and why: Savings Plans or Reserved Instances are the account
  owner's choice, not the stack's; right-sizing the instances needs their
  measured load.

## Sustainability

_Minimize the environmental impact of the workload._

- `arm64` (Graviton) uses less energy for the same work.
- Scale in when demand falls: the target-tracking policy removes instances
  down to the minimum.
- Keep what is needed, no longer: log retention by tier, backups for
  `retentionDays`, development keeping no backups, and no data stored that
  the system's needs don't ask for.
- Efficient code: no polling, no work per request that can be done once at
  start-up.
- No rule enforces these: review them.

