  once, so a run delivered again changes nothing twice. Powertools'
  Idempotency keys each run by its scheduled time and keeps the record for
  a day in the stack's idempotency table, longer than the schedule's hour
  and Lambda's hour of retries together; a failed run keeps none, so its
  retry runs again. A run's record is put under its scheduled time, so a
  run done twice leaves the same record.
- Limits: a timeout on every function, 5 minutes for a run; the
  schedule's retries end within an hour of the scheduled time, and
  Lambda's within an hour of when it took the run.
  Serverless `LambdaDefaultTimeout`: every function sets one.
{{- else if $event }}
- Idempotency: EventBridge and Lambda both deliver an event at least once,
  so an event delivered again changes nothing twice. Powertools'
  Idempotency keys each event by its `id`, which every retry at either
  level keeps, and keeps the record for a day in the stack's idempotency
  table, longer than the rule's hour and Lambda's hour of retries together;
  a failed event keeps none, so its retry runs again. Storing an item puts
  the whole item, so a producer that puts it twice leaves the same item.
- Limits: a timeout on every function, 30 seconds for an event; the
  rule's retries end within an hour of the event, and Lambda's within an
  hour of when it took the event. Serverless `LambdaDefaultTimeout`: every
  function sets one.
{{- else if $queue }}
