## Performance efficiency

_Use resources efficiently as demand changes and technology evolves._

- The newest runtime: `AwsSolutions-L1`.
- Size memory to the work (Lambda gives CPU in proportion); measure before
  raising it. Serverless `LambdaDefaultMemorySize`: every function sets its
  memory.
{{- if $go }}
- Cold starts: one static binary (`go build`, `CGO_ENABLED=0`,
  `-ldflags '-s -w'`, `-tags lambda.norpc`) named `bootstrap` on
  `provided.al2023` for `arm64`; SDK clients made once in `main` or at
  package scope, before `lambda.Start`, never per request; only the service
  packages used (`service/dynamodb`), and no heavy work in `init`.
{{- else if $py }}
- Cold starts: boto3 clients and resources and Powertools at module scope,
  small bundles (only the packages `uv.lock` pins, no dev group), no heavy
  work at import.
{{- else }}
- Cold starts: SDK clients and Powertools at module scope, small bundles
  (esbuild, minified, only the clients used), no heavy work at import.
{{- end }}
- Data access by key: `GetItem` and `Query` on keys and indexes; a `Scan` is
  never on a {{ if $queue }}message's{{ else if $schedule }}run's{{ else if $event }}event's{{ else }}request{{ end }} path.{{ if $queue }}
- Batches: up to 10 messages per invocation, gathered for up to 5 seconds,
  so one cold start and one set of clients serve many messages.{{ end }}
- Find where time goes with the production traces and the p99 duration
  alarm.
- By hand: measure a stated budget with a benchmark, and say what only a
  deployed function can show (cold start, latency in AWS).

## Cost optimization

_Deliver value at the lowest price: pay only for what you use, and know
where the money goes._

{{ if $schedule -}}
- Pay per use: on-demand DynamoDB, Lambda, and EventBridge Scheduler by
  the invocation, after a monthly free tier that a run a day stays far
  below; the dead-letter queue costs only the messages it gets.
{{- else if $event -}}
- Pay per use: on-demand DynamoDB, Lambda, and EventBridge by the event a
  producer puts (a rule's delivery to Lambda costs nothing more); the
  dead-letter queue costs only the messages it gets.
{{- else if $queue -}}
- Pay per use: on-demand DynamoDB, Lambda, and SQS by the request; the
  event source polls the queue for the function, and batching cuts the
  invocations and the polling's requests.
{{- else -}}
- Pay per use: on-demand DynamoDB, Lambda and the HTTP API, which costs
  less than a REST API.
{{- end }}
- `arm64` (Graviton) functions: cheaper per millisecond than x86.
- What production costs over development: point-in-time recovery on every
  table (by the table's size, per month); AWS Backup's storage (each daily
  backup is a full copy of the table, kept `retentionDays`, so about that
  many times the table's size), and with `copyToRegion` the transfer of each
  copy and its storage there; X-Ray for every trace past the free tier;
  twelve months of logs instead of one; and a KMS key per vault. There are
  no NAT gateways to pay for. The alarms (each per month) and the dashboard
  cost the same in both tiers. Lower `retentionDays` to pay less for
  backups; 0 keeps point-in-time recovery alone.
- Log retention limits what storage costs grow to (`infra/standard.test.ts`,
  cfn-lint I3013).
- Tags attribute every cost to its project, system and container, once the
  account activates them as cost allocation tags.
- {{ if $queue }}The event source's maximum concurrency caps what a flood of messages can
  spend; the queue holds the rest.{{ else if $schedule }}The schedule caps what the job can spend: one run per scheduled time,
  and its retries within the hour.{{ else if $event }}The producers cap what the consumer can spend: one invocation per
  event, and its retries within the hour; Lambda's account concurrency
  holds a flood back, and the rule retries what it throttles.{{ else }}Throttling caps what a runaway client can spend.{{ end }}
- By hand, and why: right-size memory and timeouts from measured load,
  which only a deployed function's metrics show; no provisioned concurrency
  or capacity unless the issue states the load that needs it.

## Sustainability

_Minimize the environmental impact of the workload._

- Serverless and managed services run only while there is work, on shared
  infrastructure.
- `arm64` (Graviton) uses less energy for the same work.
- Keep what is needed, no longer: log retention by tier, backups for
  `retentionDays`, development keeping no backups, and no data stored that
  the system's needs don't ask for.
- Efficient code: no polling{{ if $queue }} of its own (the event source polls){{ else if $schedule }} (the function runs only when a run is due){{ else if $event }} (the function runs only when an event comes){{ end }}, no work per {{ if $queue }}message{{ else if $schedule }}run{{ else if $event }}event{{ else }}request{{ end }} that can be done once at
  start-up, {{ if $go }}a small static binary{{ else }}small bundles{{ end }}.
- No rule enforces these: review them.

{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-static-site-ts" }}# Well-Architected

A digest of the AWS Well-Architected Framework, for this repository: one
section per pillar, what the pillar asks of a static site served by
CloudFront from an S3 bucket, what each tier does about it, what
`infra/operations.ts` changes, the CDK Nag rules (`AwsSolutions` and
`Serverless` packs) and the Standard's checks that enforce it, and what
stays by hand, with the reason. Sources and the date they were read are in `docs/style/SOURCES.md`.
This file belongs to the Standard: a task never changes it.

`make check` builds the site with Vite and synthesizes the stack for both
tiers, into `cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and runs CDK
Nag's `AwsSolutions` and `Serverless` checks on both (`infra/standard.ts`):
a finding fails synth unless it is acknowledged, on one construct, with a
reason.
`infra/standard.test.ts` checks, for both tiers, what CDK Nag doesn't: tags,
wildcard actions, encryption, log retention, removal policies, the versions
of the site's bucket, and every promise below. `cfn-lint --include-checks I`
lints both, with I3011 (a stateful resource without a deletion policy) and
I3013 (content that expires without a set retention).

## Tiers and the operations block

The tier is where the stack runs, and a deploy names it:
`cdk deploy -c tier=production`, or `development` when it names none. The
code never picks one, and nothing else is read at synth: a synth of a tier is
the same on every machine. A development stack's name ends in
`-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes a production
stack. Both are in us-east-1, the only region where CloudFront takes a web
application firewall and a certificate. `infra/standard.ts` (`TIER_RULES`)
says what each tier means:

| The stack                                        | `production`                             | `development`          |
| ------------------------------------------------ | ---------------------------------------- | ---------------------- |
| The site's bucket                                | kept when the stack goes (`RETAIN`)      | deleted with its files |
| Earlier versions of the site's files             | kept 35 days, or `retentionDays` if more | none                   |
| Termination protection on the stack              | on                                       | off                    |
| A web application firewall (AWS WAF)             | on                                       | none                   |
| X-Ray tracing of the function that copies a site | on                                       | off                    |
| Log retention, access logs included              | one year                                 | one month              |
| Alarms, their topic and a dashboard              | on                                       | on                     |

No tier has an AWS Backup plan: nothing in the stack is what AWS Backup
backs up. The site's source is in git, and the bucket's versions keep its
files' history in production.

What the Container promises in either tier is `infra/operations.ts`, which
the factory renders from its `spec.operations`; a change to the block is
written into the file, never the other way.

- `backups.retentionDays`: days the bucket keeps a file's earlier versions
  in production, when more than 35.
- `backups.copyToRegion`: nothing is copied for a static site. CloudFront is
  global, and the files are built again from git; `docs/runbook.md` says
  what a lost us-east-1 means.
- `recovery.rpo` and `recovery.rto`: the most of the site's files a restore
  may lose and the longest it may take, which `docs/runbook.md` is written
  to.
- `alerts.email`: the default of the stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which
  a deploy sets per environment; empty tells nobody.

## Operational excellence

_Run and monitor the system, and improve it in small, reversible changes._

- Everything is code: the stack in `infra/` is the only way resources are
  made, and the site in the bucket is only ever what a deploy copied there.
  No change by hand in the console or the bucket.
- Changes are small, frequent and checked: `make check` builds the site and
  synthesizes and lints every change for both tiers; nothing here deploys.
- One deploy, one version of the site: Vite builds it at synth, through CDK's
  local bundling (never Docker), and the deploy copies the build into the
  bucket beside earlier builds' files and clears CloudFront's cache (`/*`).
  A deploy that fails rolls back, earlier files included.
- Observe (OPS08): the distribution's standard logs and the bucket's server
  access logs go to the `Logs` bucket (`cloudfront/`, `s3/`); the copying
  function logs to its own log group, and is traced with X-Ray in
  production; the firewall keeps sampled requests and metrics.
  - `AwsSolutions-CFR3`: the distribution has access logging.
  - `AwsSolutions-S1`: the site's bucket has server access logs. The `Logs`
    bucket's own is acknowledged: its access would need yet another bucket.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts` checks every log group keeps the tier's time;
    the `Logs` bucket expires each log after the same time.
- Alarms are code (OPS08-BP04, REL06-BP03), the same in both tiers:
  `cdk-monitoring-constructs` builds them on the distribution's 5xx error
  rate (over 1 %) and the copying function's errors, throttles and p99
  duration (over 12 of its 15 minutes), each firing on one bad five-minute
  period. Every alarm notifies the stack's one SNS topic, encrypted with its
  own key, and a dashboard shows them, with the firewall's allowed and
  blocked requests (OPS08-BP05). `infra/standard.test.ts` checks that every
  function and distribution has its alarms and that every alarm notifies
  the topic.
- Nobody is told without asking: the `AlertEmail` parameter subscribes one
  address to the topic, and an empty one none. The topic's ARN is the
  stack's `AlarmTopicArn` output and the site's URL `EntryPoint`, so a
  deploy target can subscribe its own channel and find the site.
- A runbook: `docs/runbook.md` says what each alarm means and what to do
  first, how to bring a file's earlier version back and how to roll a
  deployment back. A change to what the stack keeps updates it, as a
  Standard change.
- Tag everything so cost and ownership are traceable: every stack carries
  `c4factory:project`, `c4factory:system` and `c4factory:container`
  (`infra/standard.ts`, tested).
- By hand, and why: whether the alarms fire for the right reasons, and
  which are missing (REL06-BP06), only a deployed stack's history shows;
  review it after every incident.

## Security

_Protect data, systems and assets: identity, least privilege, encryption,
traceability, and protection at every layer._

- Only the distribution reads the bucket: origin access control signs its
  requests, the bucket's policy allows only that distribution, and the
  bucket blocks every public access.
  - `AwsSolutions-CFR7`: the distribution uses origin access control.
  - `AwsSolutions-S2`, `AwsSolutions-S5`: no public bucket, no open policy.
- Encrypt in transit: the distribution redirects HTTP to HTTPS, and with a
  `DomainName` answers with its `CertificateArn`'s certificate (ACM, in
  us-east-1) by SNI, TLS 1.2 or later (`TLSv1.2_2021`). Without a domain it
  answers on CloudFront's own, whose certificate CloudFront keeps at TLSv1
  whatever is set.
  - `AwsSolutions-CFR4`: acknowledged, with that reason.
  - `AwsSolutions-S10`, `AwsSolutions-SNS3`: the buckets and the alarm topic
    refuse requests without TLS.
- Encrypt at rest: both buckets with S3-managed keys, the alarm topic with
  its own KMS key, which CloudWatch may use to publish.
  `infra/standard.test.ts` requires encryption on every bucket and topic.
  - `AwsSolutions-KMS5`: customer-managed keys rotate.
- Headers every answer carries: AWS's managed `SECURITY_HEADERS` policy
  (HSTS, no sniffing, no framing, a strict referrer policy). A content
  security policy is the site's to add, when it knows what it loads.
- A web application firewall in production (AWS WAF, scope `CLOUDFRONT`):
  AWS's managed rules for addresses with a bad reputation, common attacks
  and known bad inputs, and a limit of 2,000 requests in five minutes per
  address. It logs sampled requests, not every request.
  - `AwsSolutions-CFR2`: acknowledged in development only, which has no
    firewall.
  - `AwsSolutions-CFR1`: acknowledged; the site is for anyone, anywhere.
- Least privilege: the function that copies the site has its own role,
  allowed to write only its own log group (not AWS's managed
  `AWSLambdaBasicExecutionRole`) and what the copy needs.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM4`: no AWS managed policies on a role.
  - `AwsSolutions-IAM5`: no wildcard actions or resources without an
    acknowledgement naming the one finding and why; each of the copy's is
    acknowledged on its role, and X-Ray's write actions, which take no
    resource but `*`, too.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: no `*` or `service:*` action anywhere, but a
    key's default policy, which hands the key's use to the account's IAM.
- The page holds no secret: everything Vite builds is public. A key, a
  token or a password never goes into `src/`, `index.html` or `public/`.
- The account's own guards (GuardDuty, Security Hub, CloudTrail, AWS Config)
  are its owner's, not a stack's: `docs/runbook.md` lists them.
- By hand: treat whatever the page reads (the URL, a form, a fetched
  answer) as untrusted; write it into the page as text, never as HTML.

## Reliability

_Recover from failure, scale with demand, and keep working when a part
fails._

- Managed, global services: CloudFront serves from edge locations, and S3
  keeps the bucket's files across the region's Availability Zones. Nothing
  runs between deploys.
- Recovery objectives (REL13-BP01): `recovery.rpo` and `recovery.rto` say
  what a restore in production may lose and how long it may take;
  `docs/runbook.md` is written to them.
- Back up data (REL09): the site's files are built from git, so any commit
  is a backup a deploy restores. In production the bucket is versioned and
  keeps each file's earlier versions for 35 days, or `retentionDays` when
  that is longer, so a file changed by hand comes back too.
- Nothing of value is deleted by accident, in production: the bucket is
  `RETAIN`, and the stack has termination protection. A change that
  replaces the bucket (a new logical id) keeps the old one outside the
  stack and serves a new, empty one: never rename it.
  - `infra/standard.test.ts`: per tier, the removal policies, the
    protection, and the versions of every data bucket (every bucket no
    other resource writes its logs to) and their expiry.
  - cfn-lint I3011: every stateful resource has a deletion policy.
- Restore by a written procedure (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP05):
  `docs/runbook.md` brings a file's earlier version back, rolls a
  deployment back, and says what a lost us-east-1 means: the edge serves
  what it has cached, and the distribution is changed only from there.
- By hand, and why: a restore drill (REL09-BP04, REL13-BP03), at least a
  quarter apart, and a game day once a year; only an account shows that a
  restore works and how long it takes, so the RTO is a promise until a drill
  measures it. `docs/runbook.md` says how.

## Performance efficiency

_Use resources efficiently as demand changes and technology evolves._

- Serve from the edge: CloudFront caches the files near the visitor, with
  HTTP/2 and HTTP/3, and compresses what it serves. Its North America and
  Europe edge locations serve everyone (`PriceClass_100`); a visitor
  elsewhere waits longer.
- Build small: Vite bundles and minifies `src/` into hashed files, so a
  page loads only what it imports.
- Every deploy clears CloudFront's cache, so the edge serves the new files
  at once; a browser holding an earlier `index.html` still finds its assets,
  since a deploy keeps earlier builds' files.
- By hand: measure a stated budget (the page's size, its load time) on the
  built site, `npm run build` then `dist/`, and say what only a deployed site
  shows (load time from the edge, in a real browser).

## Cost optimization

_Deliver value at the lowest price: pay only for what you use, and know
where the money goes._

- Pay per use: CloudFront per request and per byte served, S3 per byte
  stored and per request, and the copying function only while a deploy
  runs.
- `PriceClass_100`: the cheapest edge locations.
- What production costs over development: the web application firewall (a
  monthly fee for the web ACL and each of its rules, and a fee per million
  requests); the storage of the files' earlier versions; twelve months of
  access logs and the copy's logs instead of one; and X-Ray for each
  deploy's copy. The alarms (each per month) and the dashboard cost the same
  in both tiers.
- Log retention limits what storage costs grow to: the `Logs` bucket
  expires each log after the tier's time, as every log group does
  (`infra/standard.test.ts`, cfn-lint I3013).
- Tags attribute every cost to its project, system and container, once the
  account activates them as cost allocation tags.
- The firewall's limit per address caps what one client can make the site
  serve.
- By hand, and why: the price class and the firewall's rules from measured
  traffic, which only a deployed site's metrics show.

## Sustainability

_Minimize the environmental impact of the workload._

- Nothing runs but on a request or a deploy: CloudFront serves from its
  cache on shared infrastructure, and the copy runs once per deploy.
- Small files: Vite's minified bundles, and CloudFront's compression.
- Keep what is needed, no longer: log retention by tier, the files' earlier
  versions for a set time, and development keeping none.
- No rule enforces these: review them.

{{ end }}## Acknowledging a finding

Fix the resource first. When the fix is wrong for this system, acknowledge
the one finding on the construct the finding's path names (or its nearest
ancestor you own): a finding on `{{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}InstanceRole{{ else if eq .Framework "aws-static-site-ts" }}DeployRole{{ else }}HandlerRole{{ end }}/DefaultPolicy/Resource`
is acknowledged on the role, not on the {{ if eq .Framework "aws-ec2-ts" }}launch template{{ else }}function{{ end }} beside it.

```ts
Validations.of(role).acknowledge({
  id: 'AwsSolutions-IAM5[Resource::*]',
  reason: 'X-Ray PutTraceSegments and PutTelemetryRecords accept no resource but *.',
});
```

The reason says why this resource needs it, true and specific; the reviewer
checks every one, and a vague reason ("not needed", "for now") is blocking.
